{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>He who disciplines a scorner takes disgrace for himself</b> — meaning, for himself, for the scorner is among the bent ones for whom there is no hope of repair." "<b>And he who rebukes a wicked man, his blemish</b> — returns to \"he who disciplines,\" and it is a doubling of the matter with \"takes disgrace for himself.\" The meaning: the scorner hates the one who disciplines and the wicked man hates the one who rebukes, and they expose their disgrace and say ugly and contemptible things. Another interpretation: \"his blemish\" returns to the wicked man — for himself, as if it said: he who disciplines a scorner and rebukes a wicked man for his blemish takes disgrace for himself. Its meaning: for the wicked man, all his deeds are straight in his own eyes, or he himself recognizes his blemish but does not want any man to rebuke him. It is also possible to interpret: he who disciplines a scorner — when the one who disciplines jests and shows mockery and scornfulness when he disciplines, he takes disgrace for himself, for the one who disciplines does so to his shame and disgrace, and therefore he insults him. So too he who rebukes a wicked man and exposes his blemish to him takes disgrace for himself, for the wicked man insults him; whereas if he were righteous he would not insult him, since he speaks to him by way of discipline and not by way of mockery."], :book :proverbs, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Proverbs -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/mishley/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:21:43.016219067Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}